## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical, beginner-friendly ICA design that leverages a real-world calibration signal to stabilize whitening and demixing—a common technique when channel responses drift in practice.

## Key Concepts

- Convolutive ICA with short-time framing
- Calibration-based whitening initialization
- Per-bin ICA vs. joint diagonalization trade-offs
- Cross-bin permutation alignment using known chirp timing
- Ground-truth evaluation plan excluding calibration data

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo steps for chirp-informed whitening
// 1) Estimate mixing matrix from chirp cross-correlations
const mixingMatrix = estimateMixingFromChirp(chirpSignals, micPairs);

// 2) Initialize whitening transform per frequency bin
const whiteningMatrix = initializeWhitening(mixingMatrix, freqBins);

// 3) Apply per-bin FastICA with Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization
const demixingMatrices = runFastICA(whitenedSignals, nSources = 2);

// 4) Anchor cross-bin permutation to chirp timing
const itdIldReference = computeITD_ILD(chirpSegment);
alignPermutations(demixingMatrices, itdIldReference);

// 5) Monitor condition number for source-count drift
if (conditionNumber(demixingMatrices[bin]) > 50) {
  reinitializeDemixing(mixingMatrix);
}
```

